## How this differs from the Claude Code version

Same lifecycle, same artifacts, same gates. The differences are in what can be
enforced, and they are worth knowing before you rely on this:

| | Claude Code version | This version |
|---|---|---|
| Stage instructions | Skills that load when the work matches | Rules (Cursor, Copilot) or `AGENTS.md` pointers |
| Review passes | Three subagents in parallel | In parallel where the agent has subagents, otherwise in sequence |
| Path, credential and release guards | Hooks | Hooks on Cursor and Copilot; the spec gate everywhere |
| Test protection during a bug fix | A hook that asks a model | A rule — no deterministic equivalent |
| Definition-of-done check | A hook that asks a model | A rule |
| Permission and sandbox settings | Installed | Configure in your agent |
| Eval suite | Runs on `claude plugin eval` | Principles and case shape only — no cross-agent runner exists |
| Repo-level spec gate | Optional | Installed by default |

The guards are the same scripts in both; each agent's hook configuration asks
for its own output dialect. Hook payloads are not fully documented for every
agent and tool, so trigger each guard once through your agent before trusting
it — `.sdlc/playbook/setup.md` shows how.

